  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nSYNOPSIS:\n\nSplit flow aloft over the CONUS featured a trough over the \nPacific Northwest into British Columbia with a ridge axis over \nthe Great Lakes in the northern stream. The southern stream \nincluded a trough over the Lower Mississippi River Valley to the\nwest of the remnants of Hurricane Isaias. An southerly low-\nlevel jet was located over Minnesota, northern Wisconsin, and \nnorthwest Ontario. Surface low pressure was centered over \neastern North Dakota. A warm front extended eastward from the \nlow pressure across northern Minnesota while a cool front arced \nsouthwestward over northwest South Dakota into northern Wyoming.\nAn area of showers, with occasional lightning, stretched from \nnorthwest Minnesota into northwest Ontario along the northern \nperiphery of the 850 mb low-level jet.\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON THROUGH SUNDAY NIGHT:\n\nDespite a deep layer of dry air between cloud bases and the \nground, sprinkles were reaching the ground in spots. Expect that\ntrend to continue in response to strong isentropic ascent in \nthe 700-500 mb layer. Showers with brief lightning flashes will \npersist through tonight and will gradually depart northern \nMinnesota to the north. Gusty southerly winds will gradually \nweaken tonight with the loss of daytime heating and decoupling \nof the boundary layer. The low-level jet will strengthen this \nevening as it propagates farther eastward as the surface low \nlifts into northwest Ontario and the cold front moves into \nwestern Minnesota overnight. The front will very slowly progress\neastward on Sunday and is expected to stall out over Minnesota \nand northern Wisconsin. Scattered showers will develop in the \nwarm sector near the Canadian Border. Another very warm day is \nforecast Sunday ahead of the cold front. Look for highs in the \nmiddle 60s in north-central Minnesota where clouds and showers \nare expected to the low and middle 80s from central Minnesota to\nthe Twin Ports and over northwest Wisconsin.\n\nThe cold front will push a little farther south Sunday night. \nRain chances extend as far south as the US-2 corridor by 7 AM \nMonday.\n\nMONDAY:\n\nMonday is shaping up as a transition day. The cold front is \nexpected to push farther south during the day. Both the GFS and \nECMWF feature southwesterly flow near the surface over east-\ncentral Minnesota and northwest Wisconsin during the morning \nbefore the front passes over those areas. Rain chances will \npersist over northern Minnesota. Highs will range from the low \n50s along the Canadian Border to the low to upper 70s south of a\nline from Hinckley to Mercer. Strong northeast winds are \nexpected along the Lake Superior shore in response to the \nfavorable pressure gradient over western Lake Superior. Rain \nchances increase Monday night as several lobes of vorticity \npropagate eastward across the Northland.\n\nTUESDAY THROUGH THE END OF NEXT WEEK:\n\nShowers taper off Tuesday and temperatures will be a few \ndegrees either side of normal. Surface high pressure is forecast\nto drift eastward through the region Tuesday and Wednesday. Low\nconfidence in forecast details Thursday and beyond persists. \nThe 12Z suite of guidance continues to feature two distinct \nevolutions. The GFS/GEFS features an upper-level trough moving \nacross the Midwest Thursday and Friday with widespread \nprecipitation. The ECMWF/EPS generates a cutoff low over the \nIntermountain West leaving high pressure in place over the \nwestern Great Lakes. Cluster analysis seems to lean toward the \nECMWF/EPS solutions as of this morning.",
